  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "For how many positive integers \\(m\\) does there exist at least one positive integer \\(n\\) such that \\(m \\cdot n \\le m + n?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\)",
    "B": "\\(6\\)",
    "C": "\\(9\\)",
    "D": "\\(12\\)",
    "E": "infinitely many"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "For each \\(m\\), only one suitable positive integer \\(n\\) is needed. Choose the smallest allowed value, \\(n=1\\). Then the inequality becomes\n\\[\nm\\cdot1\\le m+1,\n\\]\nwhich is true for every positive integer \\(m\\).\n\nThus every positive integer \\(m\\) qualifies. Since the positive integers form an infinite set, there are \\(\\boxed{\\text{infinitely many}}\\) such values, choice E.",
